    "notes": [
      "First public beta of ShotLama, a local-first screenshot and screen-recording app for macOS, Windows and Linux.",
      "Capture an area, a window or the whole screen from the tray, a global shortcut or the command line. The frozen-screen overlay has a loupe for pixel-exact selection.",
      "Every capture lands on a Quick Access card: copy, save, drag it into another app, pin it on top of other windows or open it in the editor.",
      "Annotate with arrows, shapes, text, highlighter, spotlight, blur or pixelate (Conceal), counters and a background tool, with full undo.",
      "Copy text out of any screenshot with on-device text recognition, and read QR codes.",
      "Capture long pages with scrolling capture (Linux).",
      "Record the screen as video or GIF, with optional microphone, system audio, camera and click highlights, then trim the result in the simple video editor.",
      "Find earlier captures and recordings in History.",
      "Features your system cannot provide stay visible but disabled, with a hint about what is missing, for example a GStreamer package on Linux.",
      "Diagnostics stay in memory. You can turn on detailed diagnostics for a session and review an export before saving or sharing it. Nothing is sent automatically.",
      "This is a beta: Linux (Wayland and X11) has been tested end to end; macOS and Windows builds are new and not yet widely tested. The Windows installer is not code-signed yet."
    ],
    "tag": "v0.1.0-beta.1-2",
